  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nA departing low pressure system pulling east across central Ontario \nand Quebec continues to help spur some cloud development this \nafternoon, but otherwise fairly calm weather is expected through \nthis evening. \n\nSomewhat dissonant to our current calm conditions should be the \nweather expected for the end of the week. Within a persistent omega \nblock, upper level shortwave troughing is expected to dig into a \nbroad ridge. This should help spur the development of a weak surface \nlow pressure system over the Dakotas through the day Thursday, tying \ninto a preceding warm front stretching from north central MN to the \neastern Great Lakes. As a warm, moist surface airmass advects \nnorthward into this frontal boundary, it should meet a plume of \nsteep mid-level lapse rates thanks to a EML coming out of the \nRockies. This should make for a conditionally unstable environment \nprimed to support severe storms if initial development can overcome \ncapping. Storm development will likely be tied to passing upper \nlevel vortices, which could provide enough lift to develop storms \nthrough that aforementioned capping. The first disturbance looks to \nmove through Thursday morning, but most guidance keeps that activity \nto our south. Then, we'll have to watch for potential storm \ndevelopment along the front as it slowly lifts north across the \nsouthern border of our CWA through the day and the environment \nrecovers from any morning convection. This could align with the next \nsynoptic disturbances through the afternoon and evening. A couple \ndifferent rounds of potentially severe storms could move through \nalong this front from mid Thursday afternoon through late evening \nbut confidence on exact storm development location is low. Any \nsevere storms that do develop would be capable of producing damaging \nwinds and large hail. A tornado or two could be possible within the \nstrongest storms as well. \n\nOvernight, a loss of instability should help diminish severe storm \npotential. Into Friday morning, areas of rain and non-severe \nthunderstorms could continue across the Arrowhead and into NW WI, as \nthe surface low pressure system pushes east across the CWA. As \nbetter upper level ridging moves into the area, precipitation \nchances should diminish through the day Friday, though high \ndewpoints will make for a continued sticky airmass. \n\nSaturday should be largely dominated by high pressure allowing for \nongoing warmth and moisture advection. This should set the stage for \nadditional ridge running upper level disturbances to take advantage \nof a primed environment and develop additional showers and \nthunderstorms at some point between Saturday evening to Sunday night. \nExact track, location, and intensity are uncertain. \n\nEarly next week, all global models are picking up a signal for a \nsurface low pressure to push out of the Northern Rockies and into \nthe Canadian Plains as the omega block breaks down. This could bring \nanother round of rain and thunderstorms to our area. \n\nAs we stay largely under the influence of broad upper level ridging \nthrough the forecast period, temperatures are expected to be \nslightly above normal with dewpoints lingering in the 60s to low \n70s.",
